{
  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The optional validation hook lacks an established host integration. Exact pinned candidates must be supplied explicitly; otherwise validation diagnostics should be marked unavailable.",
    "Perturbation sizes, repetitions, minimum history lengths, temporal constraints, tie handling, and bootstrap settings remain unspecified. Compacting histories also changes positional embeddings, confounding deletion effects.",
    "Training-position probes need causal prefix construction and target realignment after deletion. Scoring an entire history can expose interactions occurring after the target.",
    "Local perturbation generators alone do not prevent diagnostics from advancing training-loader or negative-sampler random states and changing subsequent training.",
    "Repeated checkpoint audits can overfit the fixed 200 validation users per category. The report lacks an audit budget and a prespecified rule connecting diagnostic findings to model changes.",
    "Originality 5 is plausible for integration, but potential 8 is unsupported by results. Difficulty 3 understates evaluator integration, architecture compatibility, and reproducibility requirements."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Determine how to separate history-content dependence from positional shifts and temporal artifacts, and make training probes causally valid.",
      "query": "Sequential recommender history ablation diagnostics SASRec: how should recent-item deletion, older-item deletion, and latest-item-fixed shuffling control for absolute positional embeddings, item availability, and sequence length? Compare compacted versus position-preserving perturbations and explain causal prefix construction and target alignment for per-position next-item evaluation."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Resolve implementation details that could silently change training or invalidate paired comparisons. The host-specific hook still requires explicit interface documentation.",
      "query": "PyTorch reproducible diagnostic evaluation during recommender training: how can probes preserve Python NumPy CPU CUDA random states, DataLoader generator state, negative sampling, and mixed per-module train/eval states while reusing fixed examples and candidate arrays? What limitations arise with worker prefetching and stateful samplers?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Establish an affordable evaluation protocol and distinguish actionable diagnostics from validation-set adaptation.",
      "query": "Sequential recommendation diagnostic-guided model selection with 200 validation users per dataset, four-dataset macro HR@10, and fixed 99-negative candidate sets: methods for paired category-stratified bootstrap intervals, repeated checkpoint selection bias, prespecified ablation budgets, and testing whether history sensitivity predicts improvements over simple recency baselines."
    }
  ]
}
